Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the "ability to consummate" concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
"Material Adverse Disruption" means any event or series of related events that, either individually or in aggregation, materially impairs or delays the Company's ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. However, for purposes of determining whether a Material Adverse Disruption has occurred, any adverse effect that is caused solely by the following shall be excluded:
  (i) any general economic downturn affecting all competitors in the industry;
  (ii) broad changes in regulatory or legislative conditions that impact all market participants;
  (iii) normal fluctuations in interest rates, exchange rates, and other widely affecting market conditions;
  (iv) force majeure events that are not uniquely detrimental to the Company as compared to its peers.
Notwithstanding the foregoing, any delay or impairment attributable to internal administrative errors or lack of proper due diligence shall not be excluded under this provision and will be deemed to impair the ability to consummate.

End of Agreement Text.
Choices:
